<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- gdUnit4Net engine-test wiring: [RequireGodotRuntime] tests locate the Godot
     executable via GODOT_BIN. Locally, set the path below to your Godot
     4.6.3-stable .NET editor executable.

     CORRECTED 2026-08-10: this header used to say a shell-exported GODOT_BIN
     "takes precedence" over the value below. Measured in CI the first time this
     file was actually passed to dotnet test: it does NOT. RunConfiguration
     EnvironmentVariables override the inherited process env inside the test
     host, so the Windows path below hijacked a Linux runner and gdUnit
     discovered zero tests (caught by the ENGINE_MIN_PASSED floor, red on
     passed=0). A second attempt sed'd the real Linux path in and dotnet test
     then died SILENTLY in ~3s with zero output, twice; the same value works
     via plain env, so something else in this file trips the Linux adapter.
     CONCLUSION (SUPERSEDED 2026-08-11, see below). CI runs without a settings
     file and relies on ENGINE_MIN_PASSED to catch the green-54 lie this
     file's CompileProcessTimeout guards against locally
     (.github/workflows/ci.yml, the engine step's note). If you need a different
     local path, edit the value below; do not rely on an env var beating it.

     CORRECTED AGAIN 2026-08-11: the "died SILENTLY in ~3s" mystery above was
     THIS FILE. A double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment, and the
     2026-08-10 note introduced four of them, so every
     `dotnet test -settings .runsettings` since has failed to PARSE and run
     zero tests. The old LOCAL-ONLY conclusion described a symptom of the note,
     not a fault in the adapter. Never write a double hyphen in these comments. -->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <EnvironmentVariables>
      <GODOT_BIN>C:\Tools\Godot\Godot_v4.6.3-stable_mono_win64\Godot_v4.6.3-stable_mono_win64_console.exe</GODOT_BIN>
    </EnvironmentVariables>
  </RunConfiguration>
  <GdUnit4>
    <!-- Why this exists: gdUnit4Net rebuilds the Godot project before running engine tests, and
         the default timeout for that step is too tight on a busy machine. When it expires you get

             Rebuild Godot Project ends with exit code: -1

         followed by â€” and this is the dangerous part â€” a GREEN run of 54 tests instead of ~438.
         Every [RequireGodotRuntime] test is silently DROPPED rather than failed. A suite that
         passes while quietly skipping most of itself is the worst failure mode available to a
         determinism-gated project, and it has cost multiple sessions real time being mistaken for
         a flake.

         6 minutes is far more than a healthy rebuild needs (~20s on an idle machine), so this is
         not masking a slow build; it only stops a loaded machine from reporting a false green.
         If you ever genuinely hit this timeout, treat it as a real problem to diagnose rather
         than a number to raise again.

         Sanity check when a run looks suspiciously fast: the engine suite should report several
         hundred tests. 54 means the rebuild failed and you are looking at a lie.

         SECOND CAUSE OF THE SAME LIE (found 2026-07-29): the rebuild timeout is not the only way
         to get a green 54. Two gdUnit runs in flight at once â€” e.g. this checkout and an agent
         worktree both running `dotnet test godot/tests` â€” also produce it, reported as

             GdUnit4 Godot Runtime Test Runner ends with exit code: 100
             Failed to connect: Connection timeout

         followed by the same green 54. The runner cannot reach its Godot runtime, so it drops every
         [RequireGodotRuntime] test exactly as the rebuild failure does. Raising the timeout above
         does not help, because nothing timed out.

         So: engine test runs across worktrees must be SERIALIZED. Before diagnosing a 54, check
         whether another run was live (`tasklist | findstr Godot`) â€” and note that a bare
         `dotnet test ... | tail` hides the non-zero exit code behind the pipe, which is how this
         one initially read as a pass.

         THIRD CAUSE (found 2026-08-01; a reproducible trigger for it found 2026-08-03, see the
         FOURTH block below â€” which supersedes this one's "CI only" and "wall-clock cap" readings for
         full-suite runs, and leaves the 8-test-step datum below still unexplained): the Godot runtime
         connection is cancelled mid-session and the suite is truncated, reported as

             Connection interrupted by cancellation requested
             (stack in GodotRuntimeExecutor.ExecuteCommand / System.IO.Pipes.PipeStream.WriteAsyncCore)

         Distinguishing marks, so nobody wastes hours blaming a test the way I did:
           * the BLAMED TEST IS DIFFERENT EVERY RUN â€” HumanCampaignTests, then
             Playtest3dClickThrough, then MainUiTests.ShopPanel_..., then ForgeCraftTests â€” while the
             error text is identical. Whichever test is executing when the axe falls gets the blame.
           * it is a WALL-CLOCK cap, not a per-test failure: three runs were cut at 9m37s, 9m41s and
             9m48s having completed 170, 354 and 266 tests respectively. Same duration, wildly
             different counts.
           * THE CAP IS FIXED AND INDEPENDENT OF SUITE SIZE (measured 2026-08-01 on PR #314): that
             job PASSED the whole suite, 550/550 in 3m13s, and then its 8-test silent-skip-guard step
             was cut at 9m48s with the same message. Eight tests cannot take nine minutes of honest
             work, so the runtime stalled at or near connect and the session simply sat there until
             the deadline. Any run can draw the short straw, including a trivial one.
           * MEASURED 2026-08-01, and this corrects an earlier reading of the same failure: the cap
             is NOT close to how long a healthy run takes. A passing CI engine run does all 550
             tests in ~3m30s (3m29s and 3m21s measured), so the cap sits at ~2.8x the real runtime.
             PR #311's own commit aborted at 9m48s/266 and then passed UNCHANGED on re-run at
             3m21s/550 â€” same code, same suite size. So the sick run is ~6x slower per test than the
             healthy one; the suite is not creeping up on a deadline. The ~14 minutes a green
             engine-tests JOB takes is checkout + setup + restore + build + Godot import, and only
             ~3m30s of it is the test run. Do not trim tests or raise timeouts to "buy headroom"
             that already exists, and do not read a green 14-minute job as nearly-out-of-time.
           * a commit touching ONLY .gitignore failed it while its parent passed all checks.
           * it clears on `gh run rerun <id>` with the failed-only flag, because it is load-dependent.

         FOURTH (found 2026-08-03): the truncation is RESOURCE PRESSURE inside the shared Godot
         runtime, not a sick test. gdUnit's "Detected <N> orphan nodes during test execution!" warning
         counts nodes still ALIVE when a test ends â€” leaked, and leaked into a runtime that every
         later test in the session shares. A local full-suite run shows dozens of tests leaking, many
         in the hundreds and several in the thousands (602, 625, 2094, 4188, 4861 measured in one
         run), and the session dies once the pressure is high enough. It dies in one of two ways:

             Connection interrupted by cancellation requested       (it stalls; the THIRD block above)
             GdUnit4 Godot Runtime Test Runner ends with exit code: -1073741819
                                                     (0xC0000005 ACCESS_VIOLATION â€” it segfaults)

         Both were reproduced locally on 2026-08-03, on an idle machine, with zero other Godot
         processes running. Same root, two exits.

         Two things make it worse and are worth fixing on sight, but NEITHER is the root:
           * a long-lived mount left RENDERING. Playtest3dClickThrough held a live SubViewport across
             40 in-game days of real button presses; it now disables it after mounting, as thirteen
             other MainUi tests already did.
           * SimPanel rebuilds that QueueFree children in a test that never yields a frame. QueueFree
             defers to end-of-frame, so nothing is ever actually freed. Do NOT "fix" this by awaiting
             a frame per phase tick â€” that was measured at 36s -> 5+ minutes on the sweep.
             RESOLVED SINCE, in two halves (both must stay): PanelGraveyard + the MainUi mount/unmount
             drains (#377) destroy the residue so nothing leaks ACROSS tests, and the long click-through
             playtests drain per phase tick via MainUi.DrainDetachedPanelsForTests so the PEAK held
             WITHIN a test stays bounded (was ~375k nodes / ~1.3 GB RSS â€” enough on its own to kill the
             runtime two-thirds through the suite; reproduced 2026-08-03 with the residue fix already
             in: runner exit -1073741819, suite truncated at 533 of ~800 in 9m44s, both signatures in
             the same log). The sweep asserts its own peak (PeakDetachedNodeBudget)
             so losing those drain calls fails THAT test instead of a random later one.

         What this corrects in the THIRD block, with measurements:
           * NOT CI-only. It reproduced twice on an idle local machine with NO other gdUnit run in
             flight (checked: zero Godot processes), on branch fix/bound-the-clickthrough-sweep.
           * NOT a wall-clock cap, at least for full-suite runs. Two local runs stopped after 528 and
             529 tests of 787 â€” the SAME PLACE â€” while taking 1m38s and 9m49s respectively. A clock
             cannot stop at the same test twice. That is the mirror image of the CI datum above (same
             duration, wildly different counts), and cumulative pressure explains both: how far the
             session gets depends on how much has leaked, and how long it then takes to die depends
             on whether it stalls or faults.
           * It explains why the BLAMED TEST LOOKS RANDOM without needing a sick test at all. The axe
             falls on whoever holds the runtime when it goes, which depends on order and timing.
           * It explains why CI and local disagree on the same commit â€” 787 tests there, 529 here.
             Rendering disabled lowers the pressure, so CI gets further. A local run is the harsher
             one, which makes it the better place to reproduce this.

         So when a truncation appears, do NOT start by bisecting for a guilty test. Start by reading
         the orphan-node warnings in the log: the leak total is the signal, and the biggest leakers are
         where the work is. A real fix reduces orphans; raising a timeout does nothing.

         Still open: the PR #314 datum above, where an 8-test guard STEP was cut at 9m48s. Eight tests
         cannot render for nine minutes, so that instance has some other trigger and this block does
         not claim it.

         What it is NOT: gdUnit's <SessionTimeout>. That was tested â€” with
         <SessionTimeout>40000</SessionTimeout> here, the local suite still ran all 550 tests in
         2m15s, so the element is ignored under <GdUnit4>. Do not "fix" this by tuning it.

         Note also that CI does not currently pass a settings file at all (see .github/workflows/ci.yml),
         so NONE of this file applies there â€” including the CompileProcessTimeout below. That is
         tracked separately and is the first thing to fix before diagnosing further. -->
    <CompileProcessTimeout>360000</CompileProcessTimeout>
  </GdUnit4>
</RunSettings>
